  "cause": "**Every vote is now counted and no one can form a government.** Final tallies give Benjamin Netanyahu's Likud **27 seats**, Gadi Eisenkot's Yashar **21**, B'Yachad **12**, the Joint List **11**, The Democrats **8**, Ra'am **8**, Yisrael Beytenu **7**, United Torah Judaism **7**, Otzma Yehudit **7**, Shas **6** and Religious Zionism **6** — leaving the Netanyahu bloc on **53**, the opposition Jewish bloc on **48**, and the two Arab-led parties on an **unprecedented 19, almost double their current 10** (Times of Israel, BBC, Reuters). The bloc that governed held **68 seats in the outgoing Knesset**; it has lost fifteen. **Netanyahu stays on as caretaker prime minister**, and the full official results are to be presented to **President Isaac Herzog on Wednesday**, after which he begins consultations with every faction — a process that \"could take weeks\" (BBC, Times of Israel). **The campaign has not ended; it has moved from the ballot to the legitimacy of the ballot.** Netanyahu is **stepping up attacks on the Arab parties**, and analysts and critics say the purpose is to ensure that Eisenkot \"feels unable to even consider forming a minority government with Arab help that could oust him from power\" (Reuters). Eisenkot's answer: **\"Likud and Netanyahu have set out on an orchestrated organised campaign to delegitimise the election results.\"** Herzog's warning was unusually direct for a head of state in the middle of a count: **\"We must not tarnish an entire community.\"** The precedent cuts both ways — the coalition that last removed Netanyahu, in 2021, was the first in Israel's history to rely on an Arab party, Ra'am. Arab citizens are **about 21% of the population** and have just delivered the highest turnout and the highest seat count in the country's history, into a parliament where both candidates for prime minister campaigned on refusing to use those seats (Reuters, CBS).",
