  "cause": "**A jet-powered Russian drone hit two passenger trains stopped at Dnipro's main railway station on Thursday morning**, killing **at least 14 people and injuring 14 more**, among them a 40-year-old man whose legs were amputated; the drone struck a platform crowded with commuters as a train was preparing to depart, according to the state railway operator Ukrzaliznytsia (CBS, CNN, Reuters). The same wave killed **two 15-year-old girls on a busy street in Chernihiv**, where 23 people including four children were wounded; **three people died in a drone strike on a residential building in the Poltava region**; a **Zaporizhzhia nuclear plant employee was killed by a drone**, which the IAEA condemned; a strike on a **student dormitory in Dnipro** set it alight; and the assaults **cut power to 350,000 households** (CNN, Reuters, Guardian). Ballistic missiles hit warehouses in **Kyiv** the same evening. Across three cities the total was **at least 19–20 dead** (CNN, Reuters). Zelensky, calling the station attack \"horrific\": **\"Russia is deliberately hunting civilians: on trains, at stations, in residential buildings\"** — and **\"sanctions against Moscow and its war machine must not be eased for as long as this Russian war continues.\"** EU Council President **António Costa** called the strikes \"cruel and devastating\"; Macron said what is required is \"far more than a localised ceasefire\" (Guardian). **On the same day, the one restraint Ukraine had been asked to observe was lifted by Ukraine itself.** The **Wall Street Journal** reported on Thursday that Kyiv had paused long-range strikes on Russian oil refineries under pressure from **Donald Trump** — units were told in mid-October that the campaign was on hold once missions already in flight were complete, with the pause expected to run **past the US midterms and possibly deeper into November** (Guardian, Independent). **Overnight into Friday, Ukrainian drones and missiles struck the Novokuybyshevsk refinery in Russia's Samara region, setting it on fire and forcing a shutdown** (Ukrainska Pravda, POLITICO, Kyiv Post). In **Oleshky**, the truce brokered by Melania Trump and Pope Leo XIV held for another day: Russia's human rights commissioner **Yana Lantratova** said residents had until **2 pm local time on 30 October** to leave, a humanitarian convoy was prepared, and **two people, including a sapper, were killed while de-mining** (Guardian, RFE/RL).",
