   "**Mined lanes transfer the risk to flags of convenience and to crews who did not choose the route** — the ships on the southern lane are commercial, crewed by nationals of states with no part in the war, and one carried over two million barrels with its transponders dark; the direct effect is death and total loss for private owners; the compound consequence is that war-risk premiums, crew refusals and charter withdrawals will do to the oil market what no navy can — reroute, delay, and price in a conflict that has no legal status",
   "**A war fought with mines and escorts is attrition on the world's balance sheet** — $47 billion, 23 American dead, eight months, and no stated objective a mine can satisfy; the direct effect is a Pentagon whose top planner has just been removed over leaks; the compound consequence is that strategy is now decided by whoever remains, in an administration whose stated aim (\"whenever I want it to end\") is a schedule rather than a condition — which invites the other side to wait",
